00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.300
<v José Luis><lang es>Me pongo triste.</lang>

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Fico triste, fico na fila.</lang>

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:04.052
<v Orlando>So what we're going to talk about today is the
verb <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang>, and as we listen to the dialogue,

00:01:04.052 --> 00:01:08.550
<v Orlando>Valdo and Michelle are going to be using that
verb <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> in a lot of different meanings.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.050
<v Valdo>Yeah, different ideas.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.467
<v Orlando>Normally we start off with a dialogue,

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:15.030
<v Orlando>but today let's actually kind of help people out
a little bit before we actually hear the dialogue.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:21.830
<v Orlando>The basic meaning for <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> is "to remain", "to stay".

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:23.800
<v Orlando>So if I say "stay here, don't move", you'd say…

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Fica aqui, não se mexa.</lang>

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.500
<v Orlando>Whenever you want to leave somebody's house, what do they say?

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Fica aqui, fica mais um pouco.</lang>

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.050
<v Orlando>So it means "stay", "remain".

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.800
<v Valdo>Remain here.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.820
<v Orlando>And so that'll be one meaning.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.050
<v Orlando>Also, whenever you want to say something is located
somewhere, you know, "where's the bank?" You'd say…

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:47.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Onde fica o banco?</lang> <lang pt-BR>Onde fica o banco?</lang> <lang pt-BR>O banco fica na rua tal.</lang>

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.550
<v Orlando>You know, and another little help for Spanish speakers
out there: if you can't decide if it's <lang pt-BR>ser</lang> or <lang pt-BR>estar</lang>,

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.800
<v Valdo>just use <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang>.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:54.800
<v José Luis>Oh, I see.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.770
<v Valdo><lang es>¿Es bonita?</lang>

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Ela fica bonita, né?</lang>

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:03.050
<v Michelle>Yeah, you can use it in different contexts.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:09.810
<v Orlando>So, to be located, the verb <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> can be used.
<lang pt-BR>Ser</lang> and <lang pt-BR>estar</lang>, you can use <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> instead.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.300
<v Orlando>Also, <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> is used when something becomes
something. "He got mad": <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> plus an adjective.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:21.050
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Ficou furioso, ficou doente, ficou louco.</lang>

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:26.300
<v Orlando>And so <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> is really good. And for those in Spanish,
you're going to get more of a reflexive, you know,

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:30.050
<v José Luis><lang es>Me pongo</lang>, <lang es>se puso</lang>, and the adjective.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.810
<v Orlando>So, that's what it's great for.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.300
<v Orlando>And then <lang pt-BR>ficar</lang> also has this ongoing meaning,
like "he keeps on talking", "he keeps on talking".

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.300
<v Valdo><lang pt-BR>Fica falando.</lang>

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:38.300
